   "title": "[Feature Request]: notification integrations"
  },
  {
   "comment": "_Disclosure: I work at viaSocket._\n\nThe ask for Asana/ClickUp/JIRA integrations caught my eye. If you're open to it, viaSocket Embed could handle this \u2014 it's a catalog of 2,300+ app integrations (including those three, plus Google Tasks, Todoist, Microsoft To Do, etc.) that you can show directly in Fluid Calendar's UI.\n\nYour users would connect their task apps and set up 2-way syncs without leaving your calendar. On your side, it's optional and off by default: a few env vars, one backend route to sign a user token, and one UI entry point.\n\nWould a PR adding this as an optional feature be something you'd consider? Happy to discuss specifics if it's useful.",
   "comment_url": null,
   "dry_run": true,
   "issue_number": 19,
   "issue_url": "https://github.com/dotnetfactory/fluid-calendar/issues/19",
   "posted_at": "2026-09-24T05:55:34.537021+00:00",
   "posted_at_gh": null,
   "repo": "dotnetfactory/fluid-calendar",
   "status": "draft_only",
   "title": "Feature Request: External Task Provider Integrations"
  },
  {
   "comment": "_Disclosure: I work at viaSocket._\n\nThe notification work you're doing looks great! If you want to handle more than the notification-specific providers (SMTP, ntfy, webhooks), viaSocket Embed might help with the broader integration catalog\u2014it gives you 2,300+ app integrations (Gmail, Slack, HubSpot, Google Sheets, etc.) so users can build automations between PeaNUT and other apps without you maintaining each one. We'd contribute an optional, off-by-default PR: backend route to sign a token, one UI entry point, docs\u2014nothing changes unless users enable it with env vars. Would you accept a PR like that?",
   "comment_url": null,
   "dry_run": true,
